  <figcaption id="how-it-works-alt">
    <ol>
      <li>The <strong>crop</strong> is the centre of the data model: facts that hold everywhere, at <code>/crops/&lt;crop&gt;/</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Variety groups</strong> and named <strong>cultivars</strong> belong to a crop. <strong>Growing guides</strong> are one crop in one climate region, at <code>/crops/&lt;crop&gt;/&lt;region&gt;/</code>.</li>
      <li>A <strong>city or place</strong> (a readable <code>?place=</code> link, a map pin, or a <code>/places/&lt;slug&gt;/</code> page) looks up its Köppen–Geiger climate (Beck et al. 2018) and maps it to one of 15 <strong>climate regions</strong>. <strong>Countries</strong> list those regions and the crops that have a sourced guide there.</li>
      <li>A <strong>hardiness zone</strong> for a place is derived from Open-Meteo ERA5 1991–2020 and labelled with official USDA 5 °F bounds — not an official USDA map.</li>
      <li><strong>Tools</strong>, <strong>problems</strong>, <strong>companions</strong>, <strong>rotation</strong> and <strong>seed sources</strong> are shared records, used when a source supports them. Every record names its <strong>sources</strong> and licence. The same data is available as a <strong>JSON API</strong>.</li>
      <li>Your profile, favourites and “what I’m growing” list stay in this browser — no account.</li>
    </ol>
  </figcaption>
</figure>
